 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487364 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186487402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186493903 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186524963 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525053 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186525062 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186549926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying Bayes’ Theorem to a Situation in Context" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186166482 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186166877 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6c467adb-9d0e-5b33-b81e-4959e2703535" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending Bayes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167781 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1050 1140.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167047 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "b36ed83a-cc37-5ff2-a68e-2a0e28c0b24e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1061 33287 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e6025e7-7c05-542d-a78a-f9d7e627be29" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167713 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166847 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1061 33288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "fd5da403-f379-5dee-9a50-ecafcf083c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167714 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167714 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1061 39256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7a97606a-98ac-50e6-9e3c-78ae18820b2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167713 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1061 42168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b6bd91e8-6598-5c56-9fa4-bf1144fc830d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167781 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168143 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1064 "In this lesson, we will generalize Bayes' theorem to deal with situations where the sample space $\\mathcal S$ is partitioned into a disjoint union of multiple events. \n\nSuppose we have three events $A_1,A_2,$ and $A_3$ that form a disjoint union of the sample space $\\mathcal S,$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/89/89a78ef526fcd29a59bacd4d6ae1a456a596d6616af7453f1ba530a610d9a2ac.png)\n\n \n\nNow consider an event $B$ that also lies in the sample space:\n\n![](images/da/daa96423e45f9f80897e2c2d61177eab2faff77f440921866d1b81b7ef2cfe09.png)\n\n \n\nBayes' theorem states that\n\n$$\nP(A_1|B)=\\dfrac{P(B|A_1)P(A_1)}{P(B)}.\n$$\n\nNow, the law of total probability tells us that\n\n$$\nP(B)=P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into Bayes' theorem, we get\n\n$$\nP(A_1 | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_1) P(A_1)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)} .\n$$\n\nUsing similar arguments, we can say that for $A_k$ for $k=1,2,3,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{P(B|A_1)P(A_1) + P(B|A_2)P(A_2) + P(B|A_3)P(A_3)}\n$$\n\nwhich is usually written more compactly as\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^3 P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}.\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see how this generalizes. If the disjoint union $A_1 \\sqcup A_2 \\sqcup \\ldots \\sqcup A_n$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP(A_k | B) = \\dfrac{P(B|A_k) P(A_k)}{\\sum_{i=1}^n P(B|A_i)P(A_i)}\n$$\n\nLet's see an example of how to apply this theorem." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1065 6969 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "bf17685f-9890-54cc-b8a2-15815558e87a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168143 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486628 #edb/ref 1080 "0.004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486628 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486628 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "10ee0324-47ce-520c-be1b-617fc28bde99" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486654 #edb/ref 1080 "0.06" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486654 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486654 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "1f964113-d9c4-53ce-a730-891983a25308" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486762 #edb/ref 1080 "0.066" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4dd21a5c-17af-557a-936c-d9cb120f8e34" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486783 #edb/ref 1080 "0.04" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486783 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486783 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "56ac6ed1-d21c-50b6-90e0-44a21cfc1766" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486876 #edb/ref 1080 "0.045" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486876 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486876 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8146efe8-c4c6-5ac7-a7c5-ddfd418eda3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486898 #edb/ref 1080 "0.610" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486898 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486898 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "8c32fc9e-ce9a-535b-a8b3-06c486c6cd67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486959 #edb/ref 1080 "0.602" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a059a8b3-f1ed-5de8-a334-40e4ba40f83d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486972 #edb/ref 1080 "0.688" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486972 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186486972 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "a6776cbd-ebfc-53b8-ad96-0550911cd2ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487097 #edb/ref 1080 "0.672" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487097 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487097 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec76cd0f-4509-5d07-9c06-06e62501a6f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487107 #edb/ref 1080 "0.668" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487107 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487107 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "efaec769-99d0-583b-aad4-773115f51b8c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486972 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186487107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486898 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487178 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "55e23228-7c0d-5766-b1ab-6e8ce201deb8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486783 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186486876 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186486762 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487249 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c75bc298-c38f-59f1-b1e6-e063f2062f22" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487178 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148079" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1071 "An English teacher believes that students who prefer cursive handwriting tend to perform better in essay writing. To prove his belief, he puts some students into groups of three according to their average writing grades and asks them if they usually write in cursive.\n\n- $20%$ of students have low grades, and $12%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $65%$ of students have average grades, and $15%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\n- $15%$ of students have high grades, and $17%$ of those students usually write in cursive.\n\nIf $56%$ of students are female, and $16%$ of female students write in cursive, what is the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a806821e-50df-52c7-a557-34976fdac21e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487364 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $W$ be the event that the student writes in cursive, and $L,A,H$ be the events that the student has low, average, or high grades, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(L)=0.2 &  & P(W|L)=0.12 \\\\  & P(A)=0.65 &  & P(W|A)=0.15 \\\\  & P(H)=0.15 &  & P(W|H)=0.17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union $L⊔A⊔H$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP(L)+P(A)+P(H)=0.2+0.65+0.15=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $56%$ of students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.56.\n$$\n\n- Since $16%$ of female students write in cursive, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.16.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student who writes in cursive is a female, which is represented as $P(F|W).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|L)P(L)+P(W|A)P(A)+P(W|H)P(H)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.16)(0.56)}{(0.2)(0.12)+(0.65)(0.15)+(0.15)(0.17)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0896}{0.147} \\\\  & ≈0.610,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186487249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148085" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1071 "A gambling room has three roulette tables, table $1,$ table $2,$ and table $3.$ Data of all bets of a particular evening are collected.\n\n- $30%$ of bets were played at table $1,$ and $5%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $2,$ and $6%$ of those bets were won.\n\n- $35%$ of bets were played at table $3,$ and $7%$ of those bets were won.\n\nIf $10%$ of bets were played in the first hour, and $4%$ of bets made in the first hour were won, what is the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet was played in the first hour? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f697c855-b9dd-5b7e-bb24-b23154c897b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186487402 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a bet is played in the first hour, $W$ be the event that a bet is won, and ${T}_{1},{T}_{2},{T}_{3}$ be the events that a bet is played at table $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({T}_{1})=0.3 &  & P(W|{T}_{1})=0.05 \\\\  & P({T}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{2})=0.06 \\\\  & P({T}_{3})=0.35 &  & P(W|{T}_{3})=0.07\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${T}_{1}⊔{T}_{2}⊔{T}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({T}_{1})+P({T}_{2})+P({T}_{3})=0.3+0.35+0.35=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $10%$ of bets in the entire evening are played in the first hour, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.1.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of bets made in the first hour are won, we have\n\n$$\nP(W|F)=0.04.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen winning bet is played in the first hour, which is represented as $P(F|W).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|W) & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(W|F)P(F)}{P(W|{T}_{1})P({T}_{1})+P(W|{T}_{2})P({T}_{2})+P(W|{T}_{3})P({T}_{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.04)(0.1)}{(0.05)(0.3)+(0.06)(0.35)+(0.07)(0.35)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.004}{0.0605} \\\\  & ≈0.066,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186493998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186494026 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186494049 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186494060 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186494071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186493388 #edb/ref 1045 "Extending Bayes' Theorem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [1759218649