17] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549885 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148082" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1071 "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $3.3%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, and $2%$ of female children have scoliosis, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "095773d5-faca-5dcc-94ed-85e3db151ccc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549926 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practice $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C|{S}_{0})=0.03 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 &  & P(C|{S}_{1})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C|{S}_{2})=0.033\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $52%$ of children in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.52.\n$$\n\n- Since $2%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|F)=0.02.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child with scoliosis is female, which is represented as $P(F|C).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|C) & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(C|F)P(F)}{P(C|{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C|{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C|{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.02)(0.52)}{(0.03)(0.25)+(0.025)(0.3)+(0.033)(0.45)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.0104}{0.0299} \\\\  & ≈0.348,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549823 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1069 "q-148271" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1071 "A medical center is investigating the correlation between scoliosis and sports practice in a sample of children.\n\n- $25%$ of children in the sample don't practice sports, and $2.5%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\n- $30%$ of children in the sample practice one sport.\n\n- $45%$ of children in the sample practice two sports, and $2.8%$ of those children have scoliosis.\n\nIf $48%$ of children in the entire sample are female, $2.6%$ of female children have scoliosis, and $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, what is the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1787d264-9d95-57d2-8330-7eec4c052cfe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549938 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a child is female, $C$ be the event that a child has scoliosis, and ${S}_{0},{S}_{1},{S}_{2}$ be the event that a child practices $0,$ $1,$ or $2$ sports, respectively.\n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({S}_{0})=0.25 &  & P(C∣{S}_{0})=0.025 \\\\  & P({S}_{1})=0.30 \\\\  & P({S}_{2})=0.45 &  & P(C∣{S}_{2})=0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${S}_{0}⊔{S}_{1}⊔{S}_{2}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({S}_{0})+P({S}_{1})+P({S}_{2})=0.25+0.30+0.45=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $48%$ of students in the entire sample are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.48.\n$$\n\n- Since $2.6%$ of females have scoliosis, we have\n\n$$\nP(C∣F)=0.026.\n$$\n\n- Since $46%$ of children with scoliosis are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F∣C)=0.46.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen child who practices one sport has scoliosis, which is represented as $P(C∣{S}_{1}).$\n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C)} \\\\ P(F∣C) & =\\frac{P(C∣F)P(F)}{P(C∣{S}_{0})P({S}_{0})+P(C∣{S}_{1})P({S}_{1})+P(C∣{S}_{2})P({S}_{2})} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{(0.026)(0.48)}{(0.25)(0.025)+P(C∣{S}_{1})(0.3)+(0.028)(0.45)} \\\\ 0.46 & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(C∣{S}_{1}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.46(0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1})) & =0.0125 \\\\ 0.0189+0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46} \\\\ 0.3P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189 \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{0.3}(\\frac{0.0125}{0.46}-0.0189) \\\\ P(C∣{S}_{1}) & ≈0.028\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186549819 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1069 "q-147885" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1071 "The disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ of events ${A}_{1},{A}_{2},{A}_{3},{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Compute $P({A}_{2}|B)$ given the following probabilities:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P(B|{A}_{1})=0.25, & P({A}_{1})=0.28 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{2})=0.3, &  \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{3})=0.28, & P({A}_{3})=0.18 \\\\  & P(B|{A}_{4})=0.1, & P({A}_{4})=0.22\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRound your answer to $3$ decimal places." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9c281bbc-e5bb-5c0b-9b81-0eabf770ee2e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186549984 #edb/ref 1074 "If the disjoint union ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔…⊔{A}_{n}$ covers the entire sample space, Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability gives us the following:\n\n$$\nP({A}_{k}|B)=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{P(B)}=\\frac{P(B|{A}_{k})P({A}_{k})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})}\n$$\n\nwhere $k=1,2,…,n.$\n\nIn our case, $n=4,$ and ${A}_{1}⊔{A}_{2}⊔{A}_{3}⊔{A}_{4}$ covers the entire sample space. Consequently, we have\n\n$$\nP({A}_{1})+P({A}_{2})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})=1.\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}) & =1-(P({A}_{1})+P({A}_{3})+P({A}_{4})) \\\\  & =1-(0.28+0.18+0.22) \\\\  & =0.32.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P({A}_{2}|B) & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{{\\sum }_{i=1}^{4}P(B|{A}_{i})P({A}_{i})} \\\\  & =\\frac{P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})}{P(B|{A}_{1})P({A}_{1})+P(B|{A}_{2})P({A}_{2})+P(B|{A}_{3})P({A}_{3})+P(B|{A}_{4})P({A}_{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(0.3)(0.32)}{(0.25)(0.28)+(0.3)(0.32)+(0.28)(0.18)+(0.1)(0.22)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0.096}{0.2384} \\\\  & ≈0.403.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550043 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "39ce452f-966f-5775-99e0-20fb8ca0eef9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550043 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549938 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550059 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550109 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550059 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5af77563-9292-5cfd-aaa2-e0647be6b4b1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550059 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550081 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550118 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550081 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "964218e8-bf1c-559e-ad24-e50227ec5968" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550081 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186549926 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186550109 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186550081 #edb/re